# Agent guide for {{.Name}}

This is a [Mithril](https://github.com/mithril-framework/mithril) application (Go, Fiber v3, PostgreSQL).

## Commands
- `go run .` serves HTTP on :4000. OpenAPI: `/openapi.json`, Swagger UI: `/docs`, admin: `/admin`.
- `go run . migrate up|down|status` (migrations in `migrations/`, embedded in the binary).
- `go run . routes`, `go run . resources`, `go run . openapi` print the live structure. Use them instead of guessing.
- `go test ./...` must pass before you finish.
- `go run . mcp` is an MCP server with tools: app_info, list_routes, list_resources, get_openapi,
  list_migrations, migration_status, scaffold_resource (already configured in `.mcp.json`).

## Adding a CRUD resource (preferred way)
Run `mithril make:resource Post title:string:required body:text --owner` (or the `scaffold_resource`
MCP tool). It writes `models/post.go`, a migration, and registers the resource in `resources.go`.
Then run `go run . migrate up`. Field types: string text email url int float bool time uuid json.

## Conventions
- A resource struct uses `json` tags for the API, `db:"col"` to rename columns, `validate:"required,max=200,email"`,
  and `mithril:"owner"` (rows belong to their creator), `mithril:"text"` (long text), `mithril:"readonly"`.
  `id`, `created_at`, `updated_at` and the owner are server-managed and can never be set by clients.
- Permissions are `<name>.view/add/change/delete` (+ `change_any`/`delete_any` with an owner). Never bypass them.
- Custom endpoints go in `routes.go` as typed handlers (`api.Get(a.API, ...)`) so they are validated and documented.
  `a.API` requires an access token; `a.Public` does not.
- Never put secrets in code; configuration comes from the environment (`.env` for local development).
- Return `api.Error` values (e.g. `api.ErrNotFound`, `api.BadRequest("...")`); other errors become a generic 500.

## Performance
- Framework defaults are production-tuned (sonic JSON, errors-only request logs, one ACL query per request,
  a DB pool sized to the CPUs, timeouts, graceful shutdown). Don't undo them without a measurement.
- Measure before optimizing: `PPROF_ADDR=127.0.0.1:6060 go run .`, load with `make bench` (keep-alive),
  then `make profile`. Fix the top item in the profile and re-measure with the same load.
- Keep DB round trips per request low: no query per row (N+1); load related data in one query.
- Never enable `PREFORK` if anything lives in memory (caches, rate limits, rooms, WebSocket hubs).
- macOS loopback caps benchmarks far below the server's limit: run the load client next to the server
  in the same Linux VM or Docker network before drawing conclusions.
